# =============================================================================
# DigiQuiz dev bundle — copy this file to `.env` and edit if you like.
#   Windows PowerShell:   Copy-Item .env.example .env
#   macOS / Linux:        cp .env.example .env
#
# Everything here has a working default, so `.env` is OPTIONAL. It exists to
# make one dev-vs-AWS difference explicit:
#
#   *** DEV-ONLY DEVIATION ***
#   On AWS these values live in AWS Secrets Manager, encrypted with a customer
#   managed KMS key, and are fetched at instance boot by an IAM role that is
#   allowed to read exactly two secrets. The web tier has no such role, so it
#   cannot read them at all. Here they are plaintext in a file next to the code,
#   which would be a serious finding in production and is fine on a laptop.
#   See wp5-secrets-and-iam.md for how it is actually done.
# =============================================================================

# ---- Ports ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The web tier. 8080 rather than 80 so no Administrator/root is needed to bind.
WEB_PORT=8080
# The private app tier. Not published to the host in Path A.
APP_PORT=4000

# ---- Auth -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing key for the stateless JWTs. In Path B, tools/dev.js invents a random
# one per run if this is unset; set it here to keep sessions alive across
# restarts. Any long random string will do for dev.
JWT_SECRET=dev-only-change-me-not-a-real-secret
JWT_EXPIRES_IN=2h

# ---- Database (Path A / Docker only) ---------------------------------------
# Path B ignores all of these — it uses SQLite at data/digiquiz.sqlite.
DB_NAME=digiquiz
DB_USER=digiquiz_app
DB_PASSWORD=devapppw
MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=devrootpw
